  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 135 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nNot much has changed since the previous two forecast packages. The \nupper-level ridge axis remains displaced over the southeastern \nCONUS, with our area under its western fringes. Model guidance \nsuggests that an upper trough will slowly dig south along the \nCalifornia coast before moving east through the rest of the week. As \nit does so, the aforementioned upper ridge will weaken farther east. \nThis shall induce a gradual cooling trend of about a degree each day \nthis week. Increased moisture in addition to upslope flow and minor \nperturbations in the flow yields daily shower/storm chances (10-40%) \nthis week across the western higher terrain and southeast New \nMexico. \n\nThis weekend into early next week, long-range ensemble guidance \ndepicts a shortwave trough moving in from the west. If this were to \nverify, lower heights would yield cooler temperatures and an \nincrease in shower/storm chances (NBM currently shows 20-60% \nprobabilities) farther east. However, there continues to be large \nspread among the temperature and precipitation fields. This is due \nto model disagreement on placement of the aforementioned trough and \nassociated cold front. The positioning of these features determine \nwhether our area experiences warmer/drier or cooler/wetter \nconditions. An outcome that maintains high uncertainty and we will \ncontinue to monitor trends, adjusting the forecast as new data \nwarrants it. \n  \nGreening",
